UNdata Terms of Use
https://data.un.org/Host.aspx?Content=UNdataUse (read on 2026-10-06)

Everything under this directory comes from the SDMX API of UNdata
(https://data.un.org/Host.aspx?Content=API), a service of the United
Nations Statistics Division (UNSD), Department of Economic and Social
Affairs. The API page says: "The API is governed by UNdata Terms of Use".

The Terms of Use say:

  "All data and metadata provided on UNdata's website are available free
  of charge and may be copied freely, duplicated and further distributed
  provided that UNdata is cited as the reference."

(The apostrophe in the original is a typographic one.) The terms name no
licence such as Creative Commons, and they say nothing about commercial
use, neither permitting nor forbidding it, and nothing about modified or
derived works.

The same page also says:

  "The data and metadata presented on UNdata are supplied as contained in
  the source database without any addition, subtraction, amendment, or
  modification by the United Nations Statistics Division."

  "Information about the quality or limitations of the data and metadata
  should be obtained from the organization responsible for the source
  database."

  "The United Nations Statistics Division periodically incorporates,
  without notice, revisions, updates and improvements to UNdata's content
  according to the sources' availability but undertakes no obligation to do
  so, timeously or at all."

Cite as: UNdata, United Nations Statistics Division, http://data.un.org
(SDMX API), with the dataflow and the fetch date of the run.

This dataset is not made or endorsed by the United Nations or UNSD.

The files under raw/<YYYY-MM-DD>/ are the API's answers, byte for byte;
MANIFEST.json there records their URLs, sizes, sha256 and fetch times.

The Parquet under parquet/ was made from the SDMX-CSV answers of that run:

  - the answers of a dataflow, one per reference area, were concatenated in
    the order of their REF_AREA code; rows and columns are as the API sent
    them
  - OBS_VALUE, CONVERSION_FACTOR and PRE_BREAK_VALUE were typed as
    floating-point numbers, UNIT_MULT and DECIMALS as integers; every other
    column stays text; an empty cell, and "NA" or ":" in a numeric column,
    became NULL
  - codelists.parquet and components.parquet were read from the structure
    messages (SDMX-ML) of the same run
  - no value was changed

The code that fetches, checks and converts is in the repository this
dataset was built from (MIT licence) and does not apply here.
